export const SUPER_ADMIN_GUIDE_HTML = "<h1 id=\"super-admin-guide\">Super Admin Guide</h1>\n<p><strong>This console is for running the Faida platform itself — not\nfor running a shop.</strong> Everything here acts across tenants. Your\ncustomers never see it.</p>\n<p>Signed in as super admin, you can change any business’s plan, read\nany business’s audit log, reset any user’s password, and delete a tenant\noutright. Treat that accordingly.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"contents\">Contents</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><a href=\"#1-signing-in\">Signing in</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#2-dashboard\">Dashboard</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#3-businesses\">Businesses</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#4-gifting-a-plan\">Gifting a plan</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#5-users\">Users</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#6-analytics\">Analytics</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#7-license-keys\">License keys</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#8-referrals\">Referrals</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#9-affiliates\">Affiliates</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#10-webhooks\">Webhooks</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#11-support-tools\">Support tools</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#12-platform-settings\">Platform settings</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#13-audit-and-accountability\">Audit and\naccountability</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"#14-health-and-incidents\">Health and incidents</a></li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"signing-in\">1. Signing in</h2>\n<p>The console lives on its own host (port <strong>3012</strong> in\ndevelopment) and has a separate login from the POS:\n<code>POST /api/v1/super-admin/login</code>.</p>\n<p>There is <strong>no super-admin row in the seed</strong>. The account\ncomes from environment variables on the API:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL</code></li>\n<li><code>SUPER_ADMIN_PASSWORD</code></li>\n<li><code>SUPER_ADMIN_SECRET</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your token lives in <code>sessionStorage</code>, so <strong>closing\nthe tab signs you out</strong>. That is deliberate — this console should\nnot stay open on an unattended machine.</p>\n<p>The API must have <code>ADMIN_URL</code> set to this console’s\norigin, or CORS denies every request silently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dashboard\">2. Dashboard</h2>\n<p>Platform totals at a glance: businesses, revenue, signups,\nactivity.</p>\n<p>The sidebar carries a live status dot derived from real health data —\nqueue depths for email, webhooks and eTIMS, plus the eTIMS failure\nrate:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Status</th>\n<th>Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Operational</strong></td>\n<td>Queues clear, no eTIMS failures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Degraded</strong></td>\n<td>Any queue above 10, or any eTIMS failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Incident</strong></td>\n<td>Any queue above 50, or eTIMS failures above 20%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The bell shows a platform alert feed. Critical alerts are flagged\nred, and alerts tied to a specific business link straight to it.</p>\n<p>Health and alerts refresh every 30 seconds.</p>\n<h2 id=\"businesses\">3. Businesses</h2>\n<p>The tenant list — every business on the platform, its plan, status\nand trial state.</p>\n<p>What you can do to a business:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Action</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Change subscription</strong></td>\n<td>Move a tenant between tiers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bulk update subscriptions</strong></td>\n<td>Apply a change across many tenants at once</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gift a plan</strong></td>\n<td>Grant a tier without recording revenue — see below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cancel</strong></td>\n<td>End the subscription</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Delete</strong></td>\n<td>Removes the tenant. Irreversible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Impersonate</strong></td>\n<td>Sign in as that business to reproduce a problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>View data</strong></td>\n<td>Inspect the tenant’s records</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Force update</strong></td>\n<td>Push a client update to that tenant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Audit log</strong></td>\n<td>Everything that happened inside that business</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>On impersonation:</strong> it exists so you can reproduce\nwhat a customer is reporting. You are acting as them, with their access.\nIt is recorded. Use it to diagnose, not to do their data entry for\nthem.</p>\n<p><strong>On deletion:</strong> deleting a business is not a soft\ndelete you can undo from the UI. Confirm you have the right tenant, and\nprefer <strong>Cancel</strong> when the customer might come back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gifting-a-plan\">4. Gifting a plan</h2>\n<p>Use this for partner accounts, internal use, pilots and goodwill —\nanywhere a tenant should have a paid tier without paying.</p>\n<p>Open the business, choose <strong>Gift a plan</strong>, then set:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plan</strong> — which tier to grant (defaults to\nEnterprise)</li>\n<li><strong>Reason</strong> — why. Defaults to “Complimentary account”;\nreplace it with something that will still make sense in a year,\ne.g. <em>“Partner account — pilot through Q4”</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The account is marked <strong>comped</strong>. That is the important\npart: <strong>gifted accounts are excluded from revenue</strong>, so\nthey do not inflate MRR or distort your analytics. The modal states this\non screen before you confirm.</p>\n<p>Escape closes the dialog, Enter confirms.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This replaced an older flow built on browser <code>prompt()</code>\nboxes, which could not show the tier, the price being waived, and the\nrevenue exclusion at the same time.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"users\">5. Users</h2>\n<p>Cross-tenant user administration:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>List all users</strong> across the platform, or the users of\none business</li>\n<li><strong>Reset a password</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Unlock</strong> an account locked out by failed\nsign-ins</li>\n<li><strong>Update</strong> a user</li>\n<li><strong>Delete</strong> a user</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Password reset and unlock are the two you will use daily — they are\nthe most common support request and the safest to action.</p>\n<h2 id=\"analytics\">6. Analytics</h2>\n<p>Growth, churn and plan mix across the platform. Comped accounts are\nexcluded from revenue figures, so the numbers reflect actual money.</p>\n<h2 id=\"license-keys\">7. License keys</h2>\n<p>For the offline (desktop) edition. A key is a plain code\n(<code>KPS-</code> and three groups of characters). <strong>It is not\nwhat protects the licence</strong>: the customer enters it once, in the\napp under <em>Licence</em>, and the Faida server answers with a signed\nlicence that is tied to that computer. The app checks the signature\noffline; nothing in the app can make one.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Issue</strong> a key (plan and, except Lifetime, a number of\ndays)</li>\n<li><strong>Computer</strong> column — the computer the key was\nactivated on, and when it last checked in. A key belongs to\n<strong>one</strong> server computer. Till computers that connect to it\nover the shop network need no key of their own.</li>\n<li><strong>Release</strong> — frees the key from its computer so the\ncustomer can activate it on a new one (a replaced PC). The old computer\nkeeps working until its current licence period (up to 30 days) ends,\nthen drops to Free.</li>\n<li><strong>Revoke</strong> a key — the computer stops being renewed;\nafter its remaining period plus a 14-day grace it falls back to the Free\nplan. Data stays open and backups never stop.</li>\n<li><strong>Resend</strong> a key to the customer</li>\n<li><strong>Regenerate</strong> a key</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How renewal works.</strong> Whenever the customer’s computer\nis online it renews quietly (about once a day). Each renewal is good for\n30 days, never beyond the licence’s own end date. If it cannot reach the\nserver, the owner is warned during the last 7 days, then gets 14 days of\ngrace, then the shop is on the Free plan — the same result as a lapsed\ncloud subscription.</p>\n<p><strong>One-time setup.</strong> The signing key pair is made once\nwith\n<code>node apps/api/scripts/generate-license-keys.mjs --private-out &lt;file&gt; --public-out &lt;file&gt;</code>:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Put the private key’s contents in the cloud server’s environment as\n<code>LICENSE_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY</code>, keep a second safe copy, then\ndelete the file. Whoever holds it can issue licences. It must never be\ncommitted or sent in chat.</li>\n<li>Put the public key in the GitHub Actions <em>variable</em>\n<code>LICENSE_PUBLIC_KEY</code>. The Windows installer build fails\nwithout it, so an installer that cannot verify licences is never made by\naccident.</li>\n<li>Apply database migration\n<code>20261108000000_license_activation</code> to the cloud database\n<strong>before</strong> deploying this version.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Losing the private key means no new licences for installed apps;\nreplacing the pair means every installed app must be updated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"referrals\">8. Referrals</h2>\n<p>The customer-to-customer referral programme: existing users bring in\nnew businesses and earn a reward. Review referrals and their payouts\nhere.</p>\n<p>Distinct from affiliates — see below.</p>\n<h2 id=\"affiliates\">9. Affiliates</h2>\n<p>External partners who promote Faida and earn <strong>recurring\ncommission</strong>. Unlike referrals, affiliates are not necessarily\ncustomers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Review and <strong>approve or reject</strong> applications</li>\n<li>Set the <strong>commission percentage</strong> per affiliate</li>\n<li><strong>Record a payout</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Affiliates sign in to their own portal (<code>/affiliate</code> on\nthe web app) with a separate token audience — an affiliate login is not\na POS login and grants no tenant access.</p>\n<p>These endpoints live outside the super-admin controller, under\n<code>/api/v1/affiliate/admin/*</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"webhooks\">10. Webhooks</h2>\n<p>Platform webhook endpoints and their delivery log. Toggle an endpoint\non or off. Payloads are HMAC-signed so receivers can verify them.</p>\n<p>If the webhook queue depth is climbing on the dashboard, this is\nwhere you look.</p>\n<h2 id=\"support-tools\">11. Support tools</h2>\n<p>Diagnostics for handling a customer report: inspect a business’s\ndata, read its audit log, and trigger a client force-update.</p>\n<p>The usual sequence for “it is broken for this customer”:</p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Check the alert feed and health for anything platform-wide</li>\n<li>Open the business and read its audit log</li>\n<li>Check <code>/api/v1/health/config</code> on the API for a blank\ncredential — a missing key disables its feature silently rather than\nerroring</li>\n<li>Impersonate only if you still cannot see it</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"platform-settings\">12. Platform settings</h2>\n<p>Global configuration, plus an <strong>IP whitelist</strong> (add and\nremove entries) for restricting access.</p>\n<h2 id=\"audit-and-accountability\">13. Audit and accountability</h2>\n<p>Two separate logs, and the distinction matters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Business audit log</strong> — what happened inside one\ntenant</li>\n<li><strong>Super-admin audit log</strong> — what <em>you and other\noperators</em> did across tenants</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are tamper-evident. Every consequential action in this console\nis recorded: plan changes, gifts, impersonations, password resets,\ndeletions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"health-and-incidents\">14. Health and incidents</h2>\n<p>The console surfaces live queue depths (email, webhooks, eTIMS) and\nthe eTIMS failure rate.</p>\n<p>What backing queues usually mean:</p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 50%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Usual cause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Email queue climbing</strong></td>\n<td>Resend key missing, invalid, or rate-limited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Webhook queue climbing</strong></td>\n<td>A customer endpoint is down and deliveries are retrying</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>eTIMS queue climbing</strong></td>\n<td>KRA unreachable, or a tenant’s credentials expired</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>eTIMS failure rate above 20%</strong></td>\n<td>Treat as an incident — sales are not reaching KRA</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Redis backs these queues. <strong>If <code>REDIS_HOST</code> is\nunset, jobs run synchronously with no retry</strong> — queue depths will\nread zero because there is no queue, not because everything is\nhealthy.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><em>Server installation and maintenance is in the <strong>Admin &amp;\nInstallation Guide</strong>. Day-to-day POS operation is in the\n<strong>User Guide</strong>.</em></p>";
